  "wipingFaceAfterWaking": "Gently wiping the face upon waking is from the subtle and beautiful Sunnahs of the Prophet ﷺ. It removes the effects of sleep and helps you begin your day with awareness and remembrance of Allah.\n(Muslim 763)",

  "sayingAlhamdulillahAfterWaking": "Begin your day with gratitude by saying: “Alhamdulillah alladhi ahyana ba‘da ma amatana…” — praising Allah who gave you life after sleep. This dhikr reconnects your heart to its purpose from the very first moment.\n(Bukhari 6312)",

  "usingMiswakAfterWaking": "Using the miswak after waking—especially at night—was beloved to the Prophet ﷺ. It purifies the mouth and prepares you for prayer and remembrance, combining physical cleanliness with spiritual readiness.\n(Bukhari 245, Muslim 255)",

  "cleaningNoseThreeTimes": "Blowing the nose three times after waking is a Sunnah that promotes cleanliness and refreshes the body. The Prophet ﷺ taught it as part of restoring purity after sleep.\n(Bukhari 3295, Muslim 238)",

  "washingHandsBeforeWater": "Before placing your hands into water after waking, wash them three times. This Sunnah protects you from unseen impurities and builds mindfulness in daily actions.\n(Bukhari 162, Muslim 278)",

  "sunnahOfFajr": "The two light rak‘ahs before Fajr are more valuable than the entire world and everything in it. Guard them, and you preserve one of the greatest treasures of your day.\n(Muslim 725)",

  "sittingAfterFajr": "Remaining seated after Fajr remembering Allah until sunrise brings tranquility, barakah, and immense reward. It sets a powerful spiritual tone for your entire day.\n(Muslim 670)",

  "morningAdhkar": "Whoever maintains the morning adhkar surrounds themselves with divine protection. These remembrances strengthen the heart, bring peace, and guard you throughout the day.\n(Muslim 2723)",

  "duhaPrayer": "Praying Duha is like giving charity on behalf of every joint in your body. It is a beautiful expression of gratitude for health, movement, and life itself.\n(Muslim 720)",

  "rawatibDaily": "Whoever consistently prays 12 rak‘ah of Sunnah daily will have a house built for them in Jannah. These small daily acts carry immense eternal reward.\n(Muslim 728)",

  "sunnahBeforeAsr": "The Prophet ﷺ prayed for mercy upon those who pray four rak‘ah before ‘Asr. A few extra moments can earn you the mercy of Allah.\n(Abu Dawud 1271, Tirmidhi 430 – Hasan)",

  "sunnahBeforeMaghrib": "The Prophet ﷺ encouraged praying before Maghrib for whoever wishes. It is a simple opportunity to gain extra reward before the obligatory prayer.\n(Bukhari 1183)",

  "sunnahBeforeIsha": "Between the adhan and iqamah there is an opportunity for prayer. Use these blessed moments to draw closer to Allah before Isha.\n(Bukhari 624, Muslim 838)",

  "eveningAdhkar": "Evening adhkar are a shield for your night. They protect you, calm your heart, and prepare you to rest under Allah’s care.\n(Muslim 2723)",

  "sittingAfterMaghrib": "Remaining in your place after prayer for dhikr brings continuous reward and strengthens your connection with Allah.\n(Muslim 670)",

  "qiyamAlLayl": "The best prayer after the obligatory prayers is the night prayer. In the quiet of the night, your connection with Allah becomes deeper and more sincere.\n(Muslim 1163)",

  "witrPrayer": "Witr is a highly emphasized Sunnah. Do not leave it, as it completes your night and seals your day with worship and remembrance.\n(Abu Dawud 1416, Tirmidhi 453)",

  "nightDuas": "Whoever wakes at night and remembers Allah with the prescribed supplications will have their دعا accepted. These moments are rare opportunities for closeness.\n(Bukhari 1154)",

  "sleepingRightSide": "Sleeping on your right side follows the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ and brings both physical comfort and spiritual benefit.\n(Bukhari 6311, Muslim 2710)",

  "duasBeforeSleep": "Ending your day with supplications places your trust in Allah. Your soul rests under His protection, and waking again is a mercy from Him.\n(Bukhari 6320, Muslim 2714)",

  "bismillahWudu": "Begin your ablution with 'Bismillah' to fill your action with blessing and intention, turning a routine act into worship.\n(Abu Dawud 101; Tirmidhi 25)",

  "washingLimbsThreeTimes": "Washing each limb up to three times during wudu follows the complete practice of the Prophet ﷺ and ensures both cleanliness and reward.\n(Bukhari 159; Muslim 226)",

  "khilalFingersToes": "Passing water between the fingers and toes perfects your ablution and reflects attention to detail in worship.\n(Abu Dawud 142; Tirmidhi 38)",

  "miswakDuringWudu": "Using the miswak during wudu combines purification of the body and mouth, preparing you fully for prayer.\n(Ahmad 7406; Nasa’i 5)",

  "twoRakahAfterWudu": "Praying two rak‘ah after wudu is a special act that brings great reward and opens doors of Jannah.\n(Bukhari 1149; Muslim 245)",

  "raisingHandsInPrayer": "Raising the hands at specific points in prayer follows the precise Sunnah and increases humility and attentiveness.\n(Bukhari 735)",

  "duaIstiftah": "Reciting the opening supplication after the first takbir prepares your heart to stand before Allah with focus and sincerity.\n(Muslim 399)",

  "extraTasbeehRukuSujood": "Repeating tasbeeh multiple times in ruku and sujood deepens your humility and strengthens your connection with Allah.\n(Muslim 772)",

  "duaBetweenTwoSujood": "Saying 'Rabbi ighfir li' between the two prostrations is a moment to seek forgiveness with sincerity and humility.\n(Abu Dawud 850)",

  "longerSujood": "Lengthening your prostration allows you to be closest to Allah, making it a powerful time for دعا and reflection.\n(Muslim 479)",

  "seekingRefugeBeforeRecitation": "Saying 'A‘udhu billahi min ash-shaytan ir-rajim' protects your recitation and focus from distractions.\n(Abu Dawud 775)",

  "sayingAmeen": "Saying 'Ameen' after Al-Fatihah aligns your supplication with the angels and brings acceptance of your prayer.\n(Bukhari 782; Muslim 410)",

  "fullDhikrAfterRuku": "Completing the full praise after rising from ruku fills your prayer with gratitude and proper remembrance.\n(Muslim 477)",

  "enterWithRightFoot": "Entering the mosque with the right foot reflects respect and adherence to the Sunnah in sacred spaces.\n(Hakim 1/218)",

  "duaEnteringMosque": "Saying the prescribed dua when entering the mosque opens the doors of mercy and prepares your heart for worship.\n(Muslim 713)",

  "tahiyyatAlMasjid": "Praying two rak‘ah before sitting in the mosque honors the house of Allah and brings immediate reward.\n(Bukhari 444; Muslim 714)",

  "walkingCalmlyToPrayer": "Walking to prayer with calmness and dignity reflects respect for the act of worship and increases your reward.\n(Bukhari 908; Muslim 602)",

  "usingSutrah": "Placing a barrier (sutrah) while praying protects your focus and follows the practice of the Prophet ﷺ.\n(Abu Dawud 689)",

  "repeatingAfterMuadhin": "Repeating the words of the adhan connects you to the call of prayer and brings great reward.\n(Bukhari 611; Muslim 383)",

  "sendingSalawatAfterAdhan": "Sending blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ after the adhan elevates your rank and brings you closer to him on the Day of Judgment.\n(Muslim 384)",

  "duaAfterAdhan": "Saying the prescribed dua after the adhan is a means for your supplication to be accepted.\n(Bukhari 614)",

  "makingPersonalDuaAfterAdhan": "After the adhan is a special time when duas are accepted—use it to ask Allah for what your heart needs.\n(Abu Dawud 524)",

  "sayingBismillahGeneral": "Saying 'Bismillah' before actions fills them with blessing and turns everyday tasks into acts of worship.\n(Bukhari 5376)",

  "salamUponEntering": "Giving salam when entering a home spreads peace, brings blessings, and strengthens relationships.\n(Tirmidhi 2698)",

  "duaLeavingHome": "Saying the prescribed dua when leaving your home places your trust in Allah and brings protection throughout your journey.\n(Abu Dawud 5095; Tirmidhi 3426)",

  "miswakWhenEnteringHome": "Using the miswak upon entering the home follows the Sunnah and maintains cleanliness and freshness.\n(Muslim 253)",

  "wearingRightSideFirst": "Beginning with the right side when dressing reflects love for the Sunnah in even the smallest actions.\n(Bukhari 168)",

  "duaWhenWearingClothes": "Saying the prescribed dua after wearing new clothes reminds you that all provisions come from Allah.\n(Abu Dawud 4020; Tirmidhi 1767)",

  "removingLeftSideFirst": "Removing clothing starting with the left side follows the balanced guidance of the Prophet ﷺ.\n(Abu Dawud 4141)",

  "bismillahBeforeEating": "Saying 'Bismillah' before eating invites blessings into your food and protects it from Shaytan.\n(Bukhari 5376; Muslim 2022)",

  "eatingWithRightHand": "Eating with the right hand is from the manners of Islam and reflects obedience in daily life.\n(Muslim 2020)",

  "eatingWhatIsNear": "Eating from what is directly in front of you teaches discipline, respect, and good manners.\n(Bukhari 5376)",

  "drinkingInThreeBreaths": "Drinking in three breaths is healthier and follows the Sunnah of the Prophet ﷺ.\n(Muslim 2028)",

  "smiling": "A sincere smile is charity. It spreads happiness, softens hearts, and earns reward from Allah.\n(Tirmidhi 1956)",

  "givingSalam": "Spreading salam increases love between people and is a key to entering Jannah.\n(Muslim 54)",

  "goodIntention": "Actions are judged by intentions. With sincerity, even ordinary actions become acts of worship.\n(Bukhari 1; Muslim 1907)",

  "renewingIntention": "Renew your intention regularly so your daily actions remain connected to Allah and full of reward.\n(Bukhari 1; Muslim 1907)",

  "turningHabitsIntoWorship": "Through sincere intention, even habits like eating, sleeping, and working can become acts of worship.\n(Bukhari 1)",

  "rememberingAllahConstantly": "Keep your tongue moist with the remembrance of Allah, bringing peace to your heart and light to your life.\n(Tirmidhi 3375)",

  "gratitude": "Showing gratitude to Allah increases blessings and brings contentment to the heart.\n(Bukhari 1130)",

  "reflectionOnBlessings": "Reflecting on Allah’s blessings deepens your appreciation and strengthens your faith.\n(Qur’an 14:7)",

  "leavingSinsForQiyam": "Avoiding sins strengthens your heart and makes it easier to stand in night prayer.\n(Ibn Majah 4245)",

  "fightingNafs": "Striving against your النفس in obedience to Allah is one of the greatest forms of jihad.\n(Tirmidhi 1621)",

  "avoidingInnovation": "Holding firmly to the Sunnah and avoiding innovation preserves the purity of your religion.\n(Muslim 867)",

  "tafakkurOnCreation": "Reflecting on the creation of the heavens and earth strengthens faith and brings awe of Allah.\n(Qur’an 3:191)",

  "monthlyQuranCompletion": "Completing the Qur’an regularly keeps your heart alive and connected to the words of Allah.\n(Abu Dawud 1394; Tirmidhi 2949)"
  
}

  




